The analysis above does not account for potential observation biases that can affect GW detectors. For instance, the volume within which LIGO can detect a given class of sources depends on several parameters, like the source mass and mass ratio, the distance, the sky location, or the mutual inclination of the spins. For binaries with a total mass M_{1}+M_{2}=(10-100)\\rm\\,M_{\\odot}, Fishbach and Holz (2017) showed that this volume scales with the primary mass following a power-law VT\\propto M_{1}^{2.2} and decreases at decreasing the mass ratio q. Using Figure 1 in Fishbach and Holz (2017), we extract VT and q at a fixed primary mass value M_{1}=(10,\\ 20,\\ 25,\\ 30,\\ 50)\\rm\\,M_{\\odot}, finding that such relation is well described by a power-law VT\\propto q^{\\beta}, with \\beta\\sim 0.4-0.6. In the following, we adopt a slope \\beta=0.5, which is the value associated with a primary mass M_{1}\\sim 20\\rm\\,M_{\\odot}. As we show in Appendix D, setting q=0.4 or q=0.6, i.e. the values typical of systems with a primary M_{1}<35\\rm\\,M_{\\odot}, leads our estimated merger rate to vary by less than 10%. To mimic the selection effect connected with the binary primary and mass ratio, we augment our population of NS-BH mergers to 10,000 by sampling them from the combined M_{\\rm BH}-M_{\\rm NS} distribution and, from the augmented sample, we extract 1,000 NS-BH mergers weighing the probability to select a given mass and mass ratio with the selection functions f_{M}=k_{\\rm BH}M_{\\rm BH}^{2.2} (with k_{\\rm BH} a normalization constant), and f_{q}=k_{q}q^{0.5}(see also Arca Sedda and Benacquista, 2019; Arca Sedda et al., 2020). The resulting _volume weighted_ mass distribution for BHs and NSs, and the M_{\\rm BH}-q plane are shown in Figure 4. The percentage of mergers falling inside the limiting values adopted for GW190814 remains limited to P_{\\rm LVC}=4.3\\pm 0.4% for metal-poor clusters, owing to the fact that heavier BHs have a larger probability to be sele